  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What makes a family a family?",
      "a": "Love, care and looking after each other. Families can be big or small, and people can be family by birth, by adoption, by marriage or by choice.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the Love and care",
        "What is a family?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does adopted mean?",
      "a": "Adopted means a child has become part of a new family for always, with parents who love and look after them. Fostered means a family looks after a child for a while.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the Adopted or fostered",
        "What is fostering?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a joint family?",
      "a": "A joint or extended family is when grandparents, parents, aunts, uncles and cousins live together or very close, sharing meals and chores. It is common in India, Nigeria and many other countries.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the Big joint family"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Is my family normal if it is different from my friends' families?",
      "a": "Yes. There is no one normal family. What matters is love and care, not who is in it.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is my family different?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a step-family?",
      "a": "A step-family forms when a parent marries or lives with a new partner who may have children too. You might get step-siblings, a step-mum or a step-dad.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the Step-family"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The United Nations marks the International Day of Families on 15 May each year.",
    "In many countries, including India, it is common for grandparents, parents and children to live together in joint or extended families."
